  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Elevated post-frontal winds creating choppy conditions on \n  Flathead Lake through early Sunday. \n\n- Unseasonably cold temperatures (10 to 15 degrees below \n  average) bringing high- elevation valley frost Sunday and Monday\n  mornings. \n\n- Brief warm-up early next week followed by another \n  cool, active trough late next week.\n\n\nToday through Tuesday: Early morning showers across southwest \nMontana will taper off, with primary weather impacts shifting to \nnorthwest Montana today as moisture wraps around an upper-level \nlow in southern Alberta. Precipitation will remain light, with \nvalley liquid totals under 0.10 inches and mountain amounts near \n0.25 inches; a light dusting of snow is expected on peaks above \n7,000 feet. Westerly winds will gust 15 to 25 mph today across the\nwhole Northern Rockies before relaxing at sunset, causing \nlocalized choppy conditions on regional lakes.\n\nClearing skies and drying air tonight will promote strong \nradiational cooling. Widespread frost is expected in high-\nelevation valleys (e.g., Dixie, Seeley Lake, Butte), with \nlocalized light frost reaching lower elevation spots such as \nStevensville and the western Missoula Valley by Monday morning. \nTransitory ridging will bring warm, dry conditions Monday into \nTuesday, pushing valley highs back into the 80s.\n\nTuesday Afternoon through Late Week: By Tuesday afternoon and \nevening, increasing mid-level moisture and weak instability will \nyield scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms moving south to\nnorth. High cloud bases and dry low-level air will yield \ninverted-V thermodynamic profiles, elevating the potential for \nstrong, gusty outflow winds. Activity will expand on Wednesday as \nan incoming shortwave disturbance interacts with the moisture \nplume. Wetting rain (> 0.10 inches) probabilities increase to \n50-70% across much of the Northern Rockies. Ensemble guidance \nshows strong clustering (80-90% confidence) around a lingering \nPacific Northwest trough late week, preserving an active weather \nregime with temperatures slightly below seasonal averages.",
